sessions that you can

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:11.120
go to and understand
how the infrastructure

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:13.140
itself can help you scale.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:16.850
But what I want to touch upon
is what else can Google Cloud

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:20.180
do to make sure
that you have a very

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:26.010
good experience and a flawless
peak season on Google Cloud?

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:27.637
If you just understand the--

00:06:27.637 --> 00:06:29.220
let's assume that
you guys are running

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:31.350
on an on-premise hardware.

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:33.200
Everything is
within your control.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.910
The teams that run the
support, the teams that

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:39.410
are on your network, the teams
that run your infrastructure

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:41.950
are all within your control.

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:44.520
But when you move to
the Cloud, you're now--

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:47.550
there is a sense that you
are now ceding control

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:50.910
to other companies
that might not

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:54.040
be as invested in your success.

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:57.460
And that can cause
some concerns.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:06:59.600
And we understand that.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:02.670
So what we are trying
to do is figure out

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:05.100
if we can be a trusted
partner with you guys

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:08.850
and help alleviate some of
those anxiety and concerns

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:10.853
when you move to the Cloud.

00:07:10.853 --> 00:07:12.270
So what we have
done while working

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.670
with some of our retailers
is develop the program

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:21.210
that we call Peak Preparedness
Program with one simple goal.

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:24.170
Our goal is to deliver
a flawless peak season

00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:27.318
for our retail customers.

00:07:27.318 --> 00:07:29.360
And I'll walk you through
how exactly we do that.

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:36.330
As you know, for a
great event, there

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:38.700
are three distinct stages.

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:42.240
We have a planning stage wherein
you define your objectives.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:44.940
You look at your architecture
and know exactly what capacity

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:48.570
you would need to
execute on that.

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:50.880
You then have a
testing and adjusting

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:53.730
period where you run load
test and performance test.

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:56.670
And lastly, during
the event, you

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:58.560
will monitor and tune
the system to make sure

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:01.800
that it continues to meet
the service-level objectives

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:03.650
that you have set
up for the system.

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:09.880
Let's take a sample Black
Friday, Cyber Monday, timeline.

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:12.040
And I'll walk you
through how exactly

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:14.290
we help our customers here.

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:15.790
We start engaging during--

00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:17.500
pretty early, right?

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:21.040
We start engaging towards
the August, mid of August,

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:23.350
end of August time
frame in order

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:25.720
to finalize the capacity plans.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:27.850
By this time, most
retailers have

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:31.810
an idea of how the traffic
is supposed to look like.

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:33.950
We continue to work with them.

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:37.059
And usually, we've
seen that customers

00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:39.669
have a code freeze around
the end of September,

00:08:39.669 --> 00:08:41.270
October frame.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:43.630
And then they go ahead
and during this time

00:08:43.630 --> 00:08:46.890
do a lot of load and
performance testing.

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:49.260
And this is critical
because it tells them

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:54.490
how stable the environment
is and how effective

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:58.740
the environment is in
handling unpredictability.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:03.840
And we continue to work with
retailers alongside them.

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.360
In addition to that, one of
the key things that we offer

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:09.090
is the tabletop exercises.

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:12.390
Now, what we've seen
whenever a disruption occurs

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:16.080
is it's not just a technical
issue that happens.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:19.230
A lot of times, communication
structures breakdown.

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:21.780
And what we do with
the tabletop exercise

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:26.520
is kind of simulate a
disaster in progress.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.650
Take an example of
a retailer is down.

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:30.240
Twitter is blowing up.

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:33.060
And every hand is on deck.

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:35.895
But then someone is not
available for an escalation

00:09:35.895 --> 00:09:37.280
path.

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:39.330
So what we do is we
work with customers

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:42.930
and make sure that we have a
primary mode of communication

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:44.600
that's set up.

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:47.810
And in addition to the
primary mode of communication,

00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:51.440
we also work with
fallback options

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:54.180
in order to make sure that
even if something goes wrong,

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:56.690
you always have a backup plan.

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:58.250
And the tabletop
exercises have been

00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:00.740
extremely critical
for our customers

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:04.600
to be successful on peak.

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:06.890
Once the tabletop
exercises are done,

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:09.800
we have a customer
infrastructure phase.

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:12.700
The other area where Google is--

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:14.800
where we differentiate ourself--

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:18.760
is the fact that we work with
our product engineering team

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:23.750
to enforce a code freeze
around the peak weekend.

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:27.190
So if you take the weekend of
Black Friday and Cyber Monday

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:31.270
and before that as
well, what we do

00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:32.950
is we work with our
product engineering

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:36.670
to make sure that Google does
not push any new features

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:38.440
or enhancements.

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:41.500
The idea is we understand the
effort and investment that you

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:42.850
are putting on Google Cloud.

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:45.580
And we want to make sure that
you guys have a great peak

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:46.147
season.

00:10:46.147 --> 00:10:48.480
And this is something that
is very unique to what we do.

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:53.540
Once the code
freeze is done, you

00:10:53.540 --> 00:10:55.820
don't have to worry about
Google pushing something

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:59.570
and then that causing
any disruption.

00:10:59.570 --> 00:11:01.640
After that, during
the scale down,

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:03.290
we continue to support
you all the way

00:11:03.290 --> 00:11:06.740
till the end of December when
the shopping season starts

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:09.560
winding down.

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:11.200
Now, if you've
noticed one thing,

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:13.760
I've completely skipped
over Black Friday,

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:17.750
the actual weekend
when things happen.

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:21.590
So for Black Friday, what
we do is we make sure

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:23.720
that you have access
to all the resources

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:28.230
you need in order
to be successful.

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:30.785
So for example, you have
an onsite account team.

00:11:30.785 --> 00:11:32.160
So for certain
customers, you can

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:36.060
have your account team
be onsite supporting you

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:37.910
so that you know who to reach.

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:39.840
You have one single
channel of contact

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:44.580
that you can start helping
to track all your issues.

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:47.580
We also have a
fully-staffed support team.

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:50.220
We don't want our retailers
to be in a position

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.230
where they're
waiting on a resource

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:54.880
to look at an issue
that's happening.

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:56.740
So we fully staff
our support team

00:11:56.740 --> 00:11:58.690
and make sure that
everyone's aware,

00:11:58.690 --> 00:12:00.380
not just of their
technical knowledge,

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:03.023
but also about the customers
they are supporting

00:12:03.023 --> 00:12:04.690
and what the potential
impacts could be.

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:09.570
So in addition to
all of this, we also

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:14.040
have a central
BFCM War Room that

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:17.820
is staffed by Googler's
from various organizations.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:20.300
This includes our professional
services organization.

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:22.590
This includes our site
reliability engineers.

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:24.210
This includes our engineering.

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:30.480
And you also have channels
into our executive sponsors.

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:33.060
So what this means is
when an issue happens,

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:36.660
we are able to very quickly
bring the respective people

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:38.880
into a single room
and try and make sure

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:41.524
that we can address any
disruption that's happening.

00:12:45.700 --> 00:12:50.680
So as you all know,
success is a team effort.

00:12:50.680 --> 00:12:52.270
And we understand
that customers,

00:12:52.270 --> 00:12:55.630
when moving from a traditional
on-premise kind of system,

00:12:55.630 --> 00:12:59.320
they have these concerns
about, hey, how do I manage

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:00.920
all these teams that are there?

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:02.810
How do I manage my own teams?

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:06.550
How do I manage all the teams
that are internal to Google

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:09.320
and make sure that
we all work together?

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:11.500
And that's the problem
the Peak Preparedness

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:14.550
Program is designed to address.

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:17.310
What we do is we bring
all the specialists,

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:21.330
all highly-experienced
Googlers from different teams,

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:23.430
like, for example, our
Professional Services,

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:26.360
the account teams that work
day in and day out with you,

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:30.880
our support teams, again,
highly-experienced, supporting

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:33.480
personnel, our Customer
Reliability Engineering

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:37.500
teams, and representatives from
our product and engineering

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:38.920
teams as well.

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:41.520
And all of this
with the single goal

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:45.890
of making sure that you guys
have a flawless peak season.

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:52.870
So we've talked about how
Google Cloud has a program

00:13:52.870 --> 00:13:55.720
to help customers
navigate through this

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:59.260
and make sure that they are
successful during Black Friday.

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:02.260
What I'd like to do now
is bring our customers

00:14:02.260 --> 00:14:03.970
to talk about their stories.

00:14:03.970 --> 00:14:06.860
We've got two great
customers here today.

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:10.510
We've got Kohl's, who you
all know as a very famous US

00:14:10.510 --> 00:14:11.620
retailer.

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:16.457
We also have Magalu, who is
a famous Brazilian retailer.

00:14:16.457 --> 00:14:18.040
We have got the CTO
from there to talk

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:20.140
to us about their
transformation journey.

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:28.990
So I'll first invite Jimit, the
VP of Engineering from Kohl's.

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:31.180
He leads the DevOps
and the data science

00:14:31.180 --> 00:14:35.140
division for Kohl's e-commerce
and big data platform.

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:37.240
And he's been a
great partner to us.

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:40.630
When Kohl's decided to partner
with Google Cloud in 2017,

00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:42.880
his team was one
of the first teams

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:46.282
to bring their solutions
onto Google Cloud.

00:14:46.282 --> 00:14:47.740
I'll hand it over
to him to walk us

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:51.850
through how they prepared for
it and how they plan for peak.

00:14:51.850 --> 00:14:52.690
Take it over, Jim.

00:14:52.690 --> 00:14:53.410
JIMIT LADHA: Thank you.

00:14:53.410 --> 00:14:54.493
KIRAN DAVULURI: Thank you.

00:14:54.493 --> 00:14:57.538
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:02.170
JIMIT LADHA: So as
Kiran mentioned,

00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:04.650
Black Friday, for
us, being a retailer,

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:06.130
it's a very important thing.

00:15:06.130 --> 00:15:08.440
Kohl's, as most of
you all know, is

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:10.930
a leading omnichannel retailer.

00:15:10.930 --> 00:15:14.620
We run more than 1,100
stores in the US.

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:16.720
We have stores in 49 states.

00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:18.400
And we provide a lot of--

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:27.420
we provide a lot of our
own brands, which we run,

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:29.400
and a lot of other
national brands, Nike,

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:30.990
Levi's, a lot of them.

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:36.660
And overall, we make
sure that the customers

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:41.280
are the primal, most
important people for when

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:42.090
they come to shop.

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:44.420
So all our channels
are in-store.

00:15:44.420 --> 00:15:48.870
Our omnichannel, digital,
desktop, mobile, tablet,

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:51.580
in-store experiences,
the customers

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:55.770
need to get the fast response,
the expected response

00:15:55.770 --> 00:15:57.480
that you are always ready for.

00:15:57.480 --> 00:16:01.030
And that is one thing
that we really focus on.

00:16:01.030 --> 00:16:02.770
And then we are committed to it.

00:16:02.770 --> 00:16:05.610
We also do a lot of--

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:07.110
give back to the community.

00:16:07.110 --> 00:16:09.540
Over the years, we have
done almost $700 million

00:16:09.540 --> 00:16:10.470
of giveback.

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:15.900
So we really look at
the primary thing of--

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:18.480
make the customer
prime, most important.

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:22.920
And our purpose is to
inspire and really empower

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:27.290
the families for them
to live fulfilled lives.

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:29.600
So I'll walk you
through a quick journey.

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:33.290
We have been in the e-commerce
business for quite some time.

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:37.400
But we realized back in
2016 that we need to get out

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:38.720
of the data center business.

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:40.880
How do we get out
of the traditional

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:44.790
running our own data centers,
running everything on prem?

00:16:44.790 --> 00:16:47.570
How do we manage all the
network, storage, availability,

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:48.780
everything else?

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:50.120
So we started early.

00:16:50.120 --> 00:16:52.400
We spent a lot of
time thinking about,

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:54.350
how do we break
up our e-commerce?

00:16:54.350 --> 00:16:58.490
How do we derisk ourselves so
that we can actually understand

00:16:58.490 --> 00:16:59.990
what the cloud looks like?

00:16:59.990 --> 00:17:02.040
We can take the
risk and move on.

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:04.640
So one of the things
we started was we

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:08.780
moved our search platform, which
was a traditional technology

00:17:08.780 --> 00:17:11.930
running on Oracle
technologies, to open-source

00:17:11.930 --> 00:17:13.310
solar technology.

00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:16.670
Moved it to the cloud, got
some experience from it.

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:19.280
Learned on how to manage
cloud workloads, what

00:17:19.280 --> 00:17:20.660
needs to be done.

00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:25.849
Very soon after, in 2017, we
pulled our search and browse

00:17:25.849 --> 00:17:28.650
experience, which
is all our products,

00:17:28.650 --> 00:17:30.590
how the search works, how--

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:33.860
your product detail page,
your product matrix page

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:35.390
and everything else looks like.

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:39.000
We moved all of those
also to the cloud.

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:42.830
And that is where we spent
a lot of time understanding,

00:17:42.830 --> 00:17:47.370
how do we do things
like service discovery?

00:17:47.370 --> 00:17:49.970
How do we do things
like secrets management?

00:17:49.970 --> 00:17:52.910
What does scalability
really mean to us?

00:17:52.910 --> 00:17:56.090
How do we identify which
regions, which zones do we

00:17:56.090 --> 00:17:57.810
want to run our workloads in?

00:17:57.810 --> 00:18:00.290
So it really helped
us understand

00:18:00.290 --> 00:18:03.140
how complex the
cloud can be and what

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:05.030
are the different
things that we can

00:18:05.030 --> 00:18:09.290
do to make sure that our
solution is always available.

00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:11.510
Actually, when we
started in 2016,

00:18:11.510 --> 00:18:14.780
we thought we will
go to GKE, but then

00:18:14.780 --> 00:18:17.780
very soon realized that we,
Kohl's, as an enterprise,

00:18:17.780 --> 00:18:19.483
was not ready for GKE.

00:18:19.483 --> 00:18:20.900
That's when we had
to switch back.

00:18:20.900 --> 00:18:25.670
We moved to GCE and put all our
workloads onto the Google Cloud

00:18:25.670 --> 00:18:28.910
Engine just to make sure we had
much more control over it, how

00:18:28.910 --> 00:18:31.460
do we do things,
and we managed it.

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:38.450
Soon after that, in 2018, we
brought our cart and check out,

00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:41.000
our account management,
which is the real core

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:43.550
of an e-commerce
business, brought it over

00:18:43.550 --> 00:18:45.500
to the Google Cloud.

00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:49.310
This is where we had to really
focus on high availability.

00:18:49.310 --> 00:18:53.550
We had to focus on consistency
that the user experience--

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:56.255
user could be on
any server anywhere.

00:18:56.255 --> 00:18:58.130
And they change a
password, that should still

00:18:58.130 --> 00:18:59.360
be seamless for them.

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:00.600
They can add payment.

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:01.850
It should be seamless to them.

00:19:01.850 --> 00:19:05.840
So we spent a lot of time making
sure our services are highly

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:08.210
available, very scalable.

00:19:08.210 --> 00:19:11.510
And we spent a good amount of
time learning Canary releases,

00:19:11.510 --> 00:19:15.050
blue-green releases, and all
the other things that are a must

00:19:15.050 --> 00:19:18.890
when you really move
to a cloud solution.

00:19:18.890 --> 00:19:21.320
Within that, we also realized
a lot of different things.

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:23.730
Like we say, yes,
Google is awesome.

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:26.300
But then sometimes our
needs are very different.

00:19:26.300 --> 00:19:30.800
And typical example would
be we went to Google ILBs

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:35.210
but very soon realized that our
workloads were not well managed

00:19:35.210 --> 00:19:36.120
through the ILBs.

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:38.360
We could see some
servers get a lot

00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:42.140
of more requests than the other
because the ILBs typically work

00:19:42.140 --> 00:19:44.800
on a total size of the packet.

00:19:44.800 --> 00:19:45.710
What's this for us?

00:19:45.710 --> 00:19:47.930
Our user sessions
are so different.

00:19:47.930 --> 00:19:50.100
The traffic looked
very different.

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:53.065
So we had to figure out how to
put a proxy in front of them

00:19:53.065 --> 00:19:55.190
just to make sure that our
traffic is well balanced

00:19:55.190 --> 00:19:59.750
and it works seamlessly
the way it needs to.

00:19:59.750 --> 00:20:02.360
This is where we
really realized what

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:03.800
and how do we build
for the cloud?

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:08.900
Because cloud, overall, is
a really awesome technology.

00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:11.530
But it brings a lot
more complexities to us.

00:20:11.530 --> 00:20:13.640
Think of a networking team.

00:20:13.640 --> 00:20:15.920
All of us, if we
are running on-prem,

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:17.690
we are running our
own data centers,

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:20.630
we have one or two or three
networking guys to go to.

00:20:20.630 --> 00:20:22.340
Something went wrong,
call this person.

00:20:22.340 --> 00:20:24.680
That person knows how
a load balancer work.

00:20:24.680 --> 00:20:26.930
That person knows
how a firewall works.

00:20:26.930 --> 00:20:29.750
That person knows what to do
when something bad happens.

00:20:29.750 --> 00:20:30.260
Guess what?

00:20:30.260 --> 00:20:32.480
On the cloud, that is
not really the same.

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:34.370
You open a ticket
for an SRA team.

00:20:34.370 --> 00:20:36.210
That team knows VMs.

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:38.720
That team knows the ILBs.

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:40.560
That team knows security.

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:42.560
So this is where we had
to partner with Google.

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:45.410
We had to understand all
these nuances to make sure

00:20:45.410 --> 00:20:47.900
that we are built
and we understand

00:20:47.900 --> 00:20:53.585
how degraded mode works, how we
can quickly failover and react

00:20:53.585 --> 00:20:54.710
whenever something happens.

00:20:54.710 --> 00:20:56.690
Because Google, being
the great company

00:20:56.690 --> 00:20:59.240
it is, they provide
us five nines--

00:20:59.240 --> 00:21:01.680
99.999% availability.

00:21:01.680 --> 00:21:02.180
Guess what?

00:21:02.180 --> 00:21:04.850
A one-second impact
to their network

00:21:04.850 --> 00:21:07.490
could become a 10-second
impact to my platform.

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:08.490
So we really needed--

00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:11.090
we had to understand
how to manage those

00:21:11.090 --> 00:21:12.890
and how to make
ourselves resilient

00:21:12.890 --> 00:21:15.110
because some of things
are going to happen.

00:21:15.110 --> 00:21:18.930
And we need to be ready to
understand how to react to it.

00:21:18.930 --> 00:21:21.260
Same things with a
computer platform.

00:21:21.260 --> 00:21:23.180
They manage their own VMs.

00:21:23.180 --> 00:21:25.950
There is a lot of
good CPUs, cores,

00:21:25.950 --> 00:21:28.030
everything else is
available behind the scene.

00:21:28.030 --> 00:21:29.230
But then things go wrong.

00:21:29.230 --> 00:21:31.020
How do we identify
and how do we deep

00:21:31.020 --> 00:21:35.120
dive when your server
suddenly is strapping for CPU?

00:21:35.120 --> 00:21:36.570
It's not able to scale.

00:21:36.570 --> 00:21:37.620
We had to spend time.

00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:39.630
We had to learn with Google--

00:21:39.630 --> 00:21:43.620
learn how to build the resilient
architecture that we needed,

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:48.360
how to build the KPIs, how to
build all the basic monitoring

00:21:48.360 --> 00:21:51.950
and awareness of reacting
fast to a problem

00:21:51.950 --> 00:21:53.380
whenever it happens.

00:21:53.380 --> 00:21:55.400
The other thing we
learned was stock-outs.

00:21:55.400 --> 00:21:57.580
Again, now the technologies
are getting better.

00:21:57.580 --> 00:22:00.060
But when we started,
it was very interesting

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:02.700
where if one of the zone
had a stock out, how do

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:03.680
you react to it?

00:22:03.680 --> 00:22:05.255
Do you stop building
your cluster,

00:22:05.255 --> 00:22:06.630
or you go back
and figure out how

00:22:06.630 --> 00:22:09.510
to add individual
zones to a cluster

00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:11.670
and build servers
on just one zone?

00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:13.510
So these are realities
of the cloud,

00:22:13.510 --> 00:22:15.900
which, unfortunately,
you only realize

00:22:15.900 --> 00:22:18.300
after you start diving
into it and after you

00:22:18.300 --> 00:22:19.620
start working through it.

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:23.230
But again, as I said, the
technologies are awesome.

00:22:23.230 --> 00:22:25.410
We do need to
change our thinking.

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:29.010
And we need to change how we
deal with it because there

00:22:29.010 --> 00:22:33.540
is a lot of things that we need
to be aware of to get it done.

00:22:33.540 --> 00:22:35.490
And Google, being the
great partner they are,

00:22:35.490 --> 00:22:38.730
they really helped us
through a lot of it.

00:22:38.730 --> 00:22:40.680
When come back to
Black Friday, how

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:43.590
do we ensure that our
e-commerce platform

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:48.360
is available, is ready for our
customers to transact with?

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:50.250
As I mentioned
earlier, customers

00:22:50.250 --> 00:22:53.070
are the most important
users of our system.

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:55.530
And we need to make sure
that they get the best

00:22:55.530 --> 00:22:57.630
experience, the
best performance,

00:22:57.630 --> 00:23:02.100
and a seamless check out,
seamless log in experience.

00:23:02.100 --> 00:23:06.600
So we really look at
performance from the get-go.

00:23:06.600 --> 00:23:09.450
For us, performance is
not an afterthought.

00:23:09.450 --> 00:23:12.870
It is something which is
really built into our DNA.

00:23:12.870 --> 00:23:15.600
Whenever there is a new
initiative, that starts.

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:18.030
Whenever there is a new
application that we build,

00:23:18.030 --> 00:23:21.630
whenever we look at anything
that our customer interacts

00:23:21.630 --> 00:23:24.930
with, we put a lot of
focus on performance.

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:27.850
We make sure that it goes
through the lifecycle

00:23:27.850 --> 00:23:32.400
of defining the NFRs,
understanding what the business

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:34.770
goals are, understanding
what the availability

00:23:34.770 --> 00:23:36.150
requirements are.

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:39.180
Go through the level of
testing that is required,

00:23:39.180 --> 00:23:41.040
different-- you want
to test features.

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:42.420
You want to test scalability.

00:23:42.420 --> 00:23:46.140
You want to test the whole
ecosystem in an integrated

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:46.860
view.

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:49.210
You also want to test
and certify production.

00:23:49.210 --> 00:23:50.820
You expected everything to work.

00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:52.650
You went through your SDLC.

00:23:52.650 --> 00:23:56.280
You did dev, QA, stress
and everything else.

00:23:56.280 --> 00:23:59.460
But how do we ensure that things
don't go wrong in production?

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:00.720
You want to really push it.

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:02.370
That's where we have
to think through

00:24:02.370 --> 00:24:05.130
how do we run stress
test, performance test,

00:24:05.130 --> 00:24:07.480
on an existing
production environment?

00:24:07.480 --> 00:24:09.110
So we had to go
through all of this

00:24:09.110 --> 00:24:12.570
and work through,
making sure that we

00:24:12.570 --> 00:24:15.690
are able to give a very
seamless available platform

00:24:15.690 --> 00:24:17.230
to the customers.

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:20.700
Peak, again, this is
where Kiran walked us

00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:22.350
through Google timeline.

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:25.587
For us, as I mentioned,
peak is every day.

00:24:25.587 --> 00:24:26.670
We don't think about peak.

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:29.550
And we don't wake up in
August, September, October

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:31.790
to say, OK, how
should it look like?

00:24:31.790 --> 00:24:33.660
Monthly, we do releases.

00:24:33.660 --> 00:24:37.200
We do a ton of changes in our
ecosystem in an environment.

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:39.660
And we ensure every
solution that goes out

00:24:39.660 --> 00:24:41.470
there is fully certified.

00:24:41.470 --> 00:24:43.440
It's fully validated.

00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:44.940
It is highly performant.

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:47.500
It meets the business
needs that are there.

00:24:47.500 --> 00:24:50.510
It can scale-up, scale-down, do
all the things that are needed,

00:24:50.510 --> 00:24:51.810
that is highly--

00:24:51.810 --> 00:24:55.530
it is properly certified for
the use case that are there.

00:24:55.530 --> 00:24:59.490
Sometime, June, July time frame
is when we kind of start diving

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:01.030
into, OK, business.

00:25:01.030 --> 00:25:03.180
It's time for you to give
us your requirements,

00:25:03.180 --> 00:25:05.640
give us what your
projections look like.

00:25:05.640 --> 00:25:07.850
Tell us what you need.

00:25:07.850 --> 00:25:11.283
I think most of you guys, if you
are from the technology area,

00:25:11.283 --> 00:25:12.450
business thinks differently.

00:25:12.450 --> 00:25:14.970
They think about
peak as dollar value.

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:17.730
They understand they want
to sell x million dollars

00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:19.410
worth of goods.

00:25:19.410 --> 00:25:21.270
That doesn't really
convert into technology.

00:25:21.270 --> 00:25:24.540
We spend the time taking
their requirements,

00:25:24.540 --> 00:25:26.700
converting them
to our technology,

00:25:26.700 --> 00:25:29.560
and converting them to a
measurable unit, transactions

00:25:29.560 --> 00:25:32.550
per second, queries per second,
and everything else that we

00:25:32.550 --> 00:25:33.450
need.

00:25:33.450 --> 00:25:35.550
We really do a deep
dive to make sure

00:25:35.550 --> 00:25:39.300
that we have built it and scaled
our solution to the point where

00:25:39.300 --> 00:25:41.760
it meets the business
needs, after which we

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:43.500
start running our tests.

00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:47.010
We figure out how the
traffic projection

00:25:47.010 --> 00:25:50.220
needs to look like, which
component is going to get what,

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:51.960
how scalable things are.

00:25:51.960 --> 00:25:53.100
How does failure work?

00:25:53.100 --> 00:25:55.080
How does degraded
mode work, et cetera.

00:25:55.080 --> 00:25:58.470
So we do spend a lot
of time in making sure

00:25:58.470 --> 00:26:01.680
that we have done DHA, we
have done the DR testing,

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:07.260
we have done certification, and
we have certified the solution

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:09.280
to be completely ready.

00:26:09.280 --> 00:26:12.630
We also spend a lot
of time in actually

00:26:12.630 --> 00:26:15.420
trying to make sure that we
are aware of our dependencies.

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:17.700
Being a retailer,
all of us understand

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:19.980
we have to depend on
our payment systems.

00:26:19.980 --> 00:26:21.730
We have to depend on
our fraud systems.

00:26:21.730 --> 00:26:24.450
We have to depend on all
the third-party integrations

00:26:24.450 --> 00:26:25.500
that we work with.

00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:27.490
How do we derisk ourselves?

00:26:27.490 --> 00:26:29.100
How do we make sure
that we understand

00:26:29.100 --> 00:26:32.010
when one of those systems
goes bad, what do we do?

00:26:32.010 --> 00:26:34.200
So we spend a lot of time on it.

00:26:34.200 --> 00:26:36.450
We build very clear
monitoring strategies.

00:26:36.450 --> 00:26:41.010
We build very clear
rollout strategies

00:26:41.010 --> 00:26:42.900
as well as, how do we react?

00:26:42.900 --> 00:26:45.460
When something
happens, if it's bad,

00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:47.550
how do we not think
about what to do?

00:26:47.550 --> 00:26:50.520
It should be just a
one, two, three process.

00:26:50.520 --> 00:26:53.490
So coming into
September, October,

00:26:53.490 --> 00:26:56.570
we actually run as if
we have Black Friday.

00:26:56.570 --> 00:26:58.050
We run all the tests.

00:26:58.050 --> 00:27:00.810
We certify, we make
sure we have tested

00:27:00.810 --> 00:27:04.320
individual zones, all the
zones within a region,

00:27:04.320 --> 00:27:05.520
across multiple regions.

00:27:05.520 --> 00:27:06.990
How does things work?

00:27:06.990 --> 00:27:08.820
If things go wrong,
how do we react?

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:12.210
Databases have a problem,
caching system has a problem,

00:27:12.210 --> 00:27:15.600
any subcomponent has a
failure, what do you do?

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:18.940
If your inventory system fails,
do you stop taking the order?

00:27:18.940 --> 00:27:21.390
Or do you figure out how to
put that order in a backlog

00:27:21.390 --> 00:27:22.770
so that you can reprocess it?

00:27:22.770 --> 00:27:24.750
So there is a lot
of things that need

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:27.540
to be thought through
and implemented.

00:27:27.540 --> 00:27:29.220
And we spend a lot
of time making sure

00:27:29.220 --> 00:27:31.500
that it's all done
by end of September

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:34.440
because the most
interesting thing is

00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:38.600
September is the last functional
release that we allow.

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:42.390
October and November are purely
performance only, make sure

00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:44.740
that everything works
the way it needs to

00:27:44.740 --> 00:27:50.350
and it is ready for a
Black Friday, Cyber Monday.

00:27:50.350 --> 00:27:54.230
We work very closely with
Google, as Kiran earlier said.

00:27:54.230 --> 00:27:57.290
Their PSO teams, their
SRE teams, CRE teams,

00:27:57.290 --> 00:27:58.950
they came and showed
us how should we

00:27:58.950 --> 00:28:01.360
think about the SLIs, the SLOs.

00:28:01.360 --> 00:28:05.550
How should we plan so that
we let the Google engineer,

00:28:05.550 --> 00:28:08.730
during a time of
crisis, be aware

00:28:08.730 --> 00:28:11.130
that this is the dashboard
they need to look at?

00:28:11.130 --> 00:28:15.013
Google's view of our
system is only Stackdriver.

00:28:15.013 --> 00:28:16.680
They don't understand
our functionality.

00:28:16.680 --> 00:28:19.500
They don't understand how
we have built the systems.

00:28:19.500 --> 00:28:22.140
So we had to ensure
that we have given them

00:28:22.140 --> 00:28:25.770
the right KPIs, the right
dashboards, the right awareness

00:28:25.770 --> 00:28:27.390
that when I call
somebody at Google

00:28:27.390 --> 00:28:30.510
and tell them I need help, they
can jump directly to the area

00:28:30.510 --> 00:28:32.730
where they need to look
at and help us back.

00:28:32.730 --> 00:28:35.880
So the CRE and the
SRE teams really

00:28:35.880 --> 00:28:38.400
helped us get there
so that we were

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:42.480
ready for the Black Friday,
Cyber Monday days when

00:28:42.480 --> 00:28:43.830
Kiran spoke about.

00:28:43.830 --> 00:28:48.540
We also had the teams
available in the office,

00:28:48.540 --> 00:28:50.490
on hangout chats,
everywhere else so

00:28:50.490 --> 00:28:52.770
that if something does
happen, what do we do?

00:28:52.770 --> 00:28:56.802
And as Kiran said, it was all
planned, collaborated, tested.

00:28:56.802 --> 00:28:58.260
We understood what
needs to be done

00:28:58.260 --> 00:29:01.350
and how it needs to be done.

00:29:01.350 --> 00:29:04.000
The products that we
use, as I mentioned,

00:29:04.000 --> 00:29:07.410
we run most of the
Google Cloud Engine.

00:29:07.410 --> 00:29:08.490
We run their Compute.

00:29:08.490 --> 00:29:10.020
We run their networks.

00:29:10.020 --> 00:29:15.630
We run the Bigtable, Cloud
Spanner, Big Dataproc,

00:29:15.630 --> 00:29:18.150
all the technologies
that we have,

00:29:18.150 --> 00:29:21.810
we are using purely
for e-commerce areas.

00:29:21.810 --> 00:29:24.390
Stackdriver, that's a
pretty awesome tool.

00:29:24.390 --> 00:29:27.600
Does a lot of insights,
allows us to monitor,

00:29:27.600 --> 00:29:32.310
allows us to react, allows us
to get the KPIs that we need

00:29:32.310 --> 00:29:35.310
to be able to help understand
where things are going

00:29:35.310 --> 00:29:38.090
and how things are working out.

00:29:38.090 --> 00:29:38.840
What's next?

00:29:38.840 --> 00:29:44.280
I think the things that we are
focusing on this year is cost.

00:29:44.280 --> 00:29:45.770
I did skip through it.

00:29:45.770 --> 00:29:48.230
But cost is a very
important problem.

00:29:48.230 --> 00:29:50.910
Cloud makes it very easy
to say, yes, it's Cloud.

00:29:50.910 --> 00:29:51.910
Everything is available.

00:29:51.910 --> 00:29:53.940
Flip a switch and you
have your servers.

00:29:53.940 --> 00:29:54.440
Guess what?

00:29:54.440 --> 00:29:57.390
If you leave the switch on,
you keep on paying for it.

00:29:57.390 --> 00:29:59.000
We need to realize
how to manage this.

00:29:59.000 --> 00:30:01.310
In our old data
center ways, we never

00:30:01.310 --> 00:30:03.230
really cared about licensing.

00:30:03.230 --> 00:30:04.370
It was enterprise licenses.

00:30:04.370 --> 00:30:06.260
Stand up as many
servers as you want.

00:30:06.260 --> 00:30:06.840
Guess what?

00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:08.950
When you come to the
Cloud, that might change.

00:30:08.950 --> 00:30:12.930
So you need to have a primary
area and a primary focus on it.

00:30:12.930 --> 00:30:15.820
So we're going to spend more
time on cost management.

00:30:15.820 --> 00:30:18.170
We are also going to look
at the newer technologies

00:30:18.170 --> 00:30:23.240
that Google has, purely GKE,
look at their Dataproc versus

00:30:23.240 --> 00:30:26.690
BigQuery, look at areas
that we can now learn

00:30:26.690 --> 00:30:28.400
and grow to a newer one.

00:30:28.400 --> 00:30:31.040
We want to spend
more time on AIOps.

00:30:31.040 --> 00:30:33.050
That is something which
I'm assuming all of us

00:30:33.050 --> 00:30:35.090
really want to get
to is, how do I

00:30:35.090 --> 00:30:37.040
not get into managing
1,000 servers

00:30:37.040 --> 00:30:38.900
where somebody has
to know what to do?

00:30:38.900 --> 00:30:40.670
How do I have a
robot, a bot running

00:30:40.670 --> 00:30:42.845
behind the scene
which can react and do

00:30:42.845 --> 00:30:43.970
the things that are needed?

00:30:43.970 --> 00:30:45.920
How do I scale my
system down every night?

00:30:45.920 --> 00:30:48.060
How do I scale it
up in the morning?

00:30:48.060 --> 00:30:50.250
So those are the areas
we are focusing on.

00:30:50.250 --> 00:30:52.910
And then scale our
API platform, A we

00:30:52.910 --> 00:30:56.310
need to ensure that the
API platform, which we use,

00:30:56.310 --> 00:31:01.640
which is also Google Apigee,
is highly available and ready

00:31:01.640 --> 00:31:04.790
for us for peak.

00:31:04.790 --> 00:31:06.770
Let me get Kiran
back on the screen.

00:31:06.770 --> 00:31:08.174
Thank you.

00:31:08.174 --> 00:31:09.019
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:09.019 --> 00:31:10.102
KIRAN DAVULURI: Thank you.

00:31:10.102 --> 00:31:11.060
JIMIT LADHA: Thank you.

00:31:13.635 --> 00:31:15.010
KIRAN DAVULURI:
Thank you, Jimit.

00:31:15.010 --> 00:31:17.440
I think that was a
great story of how

00:31:17.440 --> 00:31:19.300
you partnered with Google.

00:31:19.300 --> 00:31:20.180
We learned a lot.

00:31:20.180 --> 00:31:21.620
We learned a lot together.

00:31:21.620 --> 00:31:23.260
And you're on Google Cloud.

00:31:23.260 --> 00:31:27.490
And thank you for being
a great partner to us.

00:31:27.490 --> 00:31:31.540
I'd now like to introduce Andre
Fatala, the CTO of Magazine

00:31:31.540 --> 00:31:32.610
Luiza.

00:31:32.610 --> 00:31:35.740
Fatala, as he is known,
is responsible for leading

00:31:35.740 --> 00:31:39.760
the technology and the
digital transformation effort

00:31:39.760 --> 00:31:43.430
alongside the CEO, Fred Trajano.

00:31:43.430 --> 00:31:45.580
And he has been the
primary architect

00:31:45.580 --> 00:31:48.670
for the digital transformation.

00:31:48.670 --> 00:31:50.290
Magazine Luiza has
transformed from

00:31:50.290 --> 00:31:55.870
a brick-and-mortar retailer,
who had an e-commerce presence,

00:31:55.870 --> 00:31:59.380
into an industry-leading
digital platform.

00:31:59.380 --> 00:32:02.810
And Magazine Luiza, or
Magalu, as it's known,

00:32:02.810 --> 00:32:05.320
is seeing a double-digit
growth today

00:32:05.320 --> 00:32:07.990
compared to the other
Brazilian retailers.

00:32:07.990 --> 00:32:10.660
In addition to
that, it's not only

00:32:10.660 --> 00:32:12.850
seeing a double-digit
growth, it's

00:32:12.850 --> 00:32:16.180
also holding its own against all
of the other digital commerce

00:32:16.180 --> 00:32:19.880
giants that are trying to
enter the Brazilian market.

00:32:19.880 --> 00:32:21.070
So I'll hand it over to him.

00:32:21.070 --> 00:32:23.740
And then he'll talk to us
about all the transformation

00:32:23.740 --> 00:32:24.490
that they've done.

00:32:24.490 --> 00:32:25.484
You have the mic?

00:32:25.484 --> 00:32:27.472
Here, you can have this.

00:32:27.472 --> 00:32:31.460
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:31.460 --> 00:32:34.572
ANDRE FATALA: So
hello, everyone.

00:32:34.572 --> 00:32:38.220
In Magalu, our approach is
a business approach for us

00:32:38.220 --> 00:32:39.990
and driven by technology.

00:32:39.990 --> 00:32:42.510
So over the past
five years, we have

00:32:42.510 --> 00:32:45.810
done a complete transformation,
the company and all the things

00:32:45.810 --> 00:32:50.520
about omnichannel, like pickup
in store, last mile delivery,

00:32:50.520 --> 00:32:52.500
mobile apps, our
sales associates,

00:32:52.500 --> 00:32:56.160
and our own
marketplace platform.

00:32:56.160 --> 00:32:58.170
And we were able
to do this quickly

00:32:58.170 --> 00:33:02.050
because we used a hybrid
approach based on APIs

00:33:02.050 --> 00:33:05.040
and microservice in the company.

00:33:05.040 --> 00:33:09.300
So we have a great results too.

00:33:09.300 --> 00:33:13.620
One thing to point out is we
did them both, in the stores,

00:33:13.620 --> 00:33:17.080
in our e-commerce
platform together.

00:33:17.080 --> 00:33:23.580
And we have our GMV of
digital around 40% right now.

00:33:23.580 --> 00:33:26.940
And in the last three years,
the stock price of the company

00:33:26.940 --> 00:33:30.220
grew 106 times.

00:33:30.220 --> 00:33:33.810
It's pretty much
the way that we see

00:33:33.810 --> 00:33:37.140
the transformation of
the company from 2015

00:33:37.140 --> 00:33:39.490
to what we have now.

00:33:39.490 --> 00:33:42.990
So talking about
the Cloud, that's

00:33:42.990 --> 00:33:44.370
our journey to the Cloud.

00:33:44.370 --> 00:33:51.180
Actually, we start in 2011
when we found R&amp;D team

00:33:51.180 --> 00:33:56.400
to start playing around with new
technologies in a different way

00:33:56.400 --> 00:34:00.060
of development inside
Magazine Luiza.

00:34:00.060 --> 00:34:03.060
And we understand
that the Cloud is not

00:34:03.060 --> 00:34:06.570
about only to reduce
costs and scalability.

00:34:06.570 --> 00:34:08.880
The idea is it's--

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:11.730
although it's a benefit
too, but the reason

00:34:11.730 --> 00:34:16.199
is that we need to empower
developers the way that we see.

00:34:16.199 --> 00:34:19.590
And we need to be able
to create fast and drive

00:34:19.590 --> 00:34:21.179
our business really fast.

00:34:21.179 --> 00:34:26.969
So last year, we decide to
do not just a hybrid but also

00:34:26.969 --> 00:34:28.590
on multicloud strategy.

00:34:28.590 --> 00:34:34.199
And we already using Apigee
and Kubernetes for some time.

00:34:34.199 --> 00:34:38.239
But last year, we decide to move
all the data workloads to GCP.

00:34:38.239 --> 00:34:41.969
And we were very happy with
the applications on GCP.

00:34:41.969 --> 00:34:44.280
And it's been great so far.

00:34:44.280 --> 00:34:51.570
And then we decide to move our
e-commerce platform as well.

00:34:51.570 --> 00:34:57.060
So one of the really critical
piece of this strategy

00:34:57.060 --> 00:35:00.080
has been the Apigee
API management.

00:35:00.080 --> 00:35:02.200
It's not just infrastructure.

00:35:02.200 --> 00:35:07.410
It's a critical strategy to give
the speed, agility, and control

00:35:07.410 --> 00:35:09.510
so we can serve our
customers and manage

00:35:09.510 --> 00:35:12.870
business while at the same time
make life easier to development

00:35:12.870 --> 00:35:13.930
teams.

00:35:13.930 --> 00:35:17.310
And that's a pretty much
the growth of API calls

00:35:17.310 --> 00:35:26.950
that we have on our platform
since 2017, beginning of 2017.

00:35:26.950 --> 00:35:33.340
Then after playing around
the data workloads on GCP,

00:35:33.340 --> 00:35:36.010
we decide to do
something really crazy.

00:35:36.010 --> 00:35:39.740
We decide to migrate our
e-commerce platform apps to GCP

00:35:39.740 --> 00:35:43.210
just 60 days before
Black Friday.

00:35:43.210 --> 00:35:45.160
And why here we
decide to do this

00:35:45.160 --> 00:35:47.450
is because we really
believe that it

00:35:47.450 --> 00:35:51.850
would have the best Black Friday
performance running on GCP.

00:35:51.850 --> 00:35:57.910
And we worked really tight with
a Google team, the PSO team.

00:35:57.910 --> 00:36:00.940
We have teams onsite
on Black Friday,

00:36:00.940 --> 00:36:04.870
help us to support all the
operation on the Black Friday.

00:36:04.870 --> 00:36:13.200
And we migrate 113
apps in just 53 days.

00:36:13.200 --> 00:36:13.770
Yeah.

00:36:13.770 --> 00:36:18.240
And it's a lot of hard
work from the teams.

00:36:18.240 --> 00:36:19.930
But it's paid off.

00:36:19.930 --> 00:36:22.980
It was our biggest and
easiest Black Friday

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:25.860
ever in the history
of the company.

00:36:25.860 --> 00:36:31.050
The sales grew around 65%.

00:36:31.050 --> 00:36:36.090
We have a peak of close
to 2,000 orders per minute

00:36:36.090 --> 00:36:41.200
and all of huge numbers.

00:36:41.200 --> 00:36:45.940
So how that possible for
us to move so quickly?

00:36:45.940 --> 00:36:50.100
It's because we are
really believers

00:36:50.100 --> 00:36:54.540
of the Kubernetes since 2016.

00:36:54.540 --> 00:36:56.310
And it's enables to
move really quickly

00:36:56.310 --> 00:37:00.680
to hybrid and
multicloud strategy.

00:37:00.680 --> 00:37:05.580
We built, in 2016, a platform
as a service call Teresa.

00:37:05.580 --> 00:37:09.750
It's open-source if someone
is interested to help

00:37:09.750 --> 00:37:15.060
us or collaborate to
continue to evolve our PaaS.

00:37:15.060 --> 00:37:18.600
And because of Teresa, we
we're able to just create

00:37:18.600 --> 00:37:22.350
the clusters, the
Kubernetes clusters on GCP

00:37:22.350 --> 00:37:27.140
and start moving our
workloads really fast.

00:37:27.140 --> 00:37:29.620
So that's a little
bit of numbers

00:37:29.620 --> 00:37:32.290
off our stack on
GCP and the numbers

00:37:32.290 --> 00:37:34.640
that we had in the
peaks of Black Friday.

00:37:34.640 --> 00:37:41.120
So we reached almost
8,000 pods on the peak

00:37:41.120 --> 00:37:46.360
and more than 44 petabytes
of logging in that month.

00:37:46.360 --> 00:37:50.990
So it's a pretty much really
good numbers of this stack.

00:37:54.160 --> 00:37:57.020
About products
we're using today,

00:37:57.020 --> 00:37:59.830
it's a lot of GCP service.

00:37:59.830 --> 00:38:02.500
And we are using all
GCP service today

00:38:02.500 --> 00:38:07.660
because I don't want my teams
thinking about infrastructure.

00:38:07.660 --> 00:38:10.540
We want to focus on
delivering value to customers

00:38:10.540 --> 00:38:13.510
and build software to
drive our strategy forward.

00:38:13.510 --> 00:38:16.720
And GCP has been a
great partner on these.

00:38:16.720 --> 00:38:24.670
And we can use what we need and
the time that we think that is

00:38:24.670 --> 00:38:28.200
the best time to this strategy.

00:38:28.200 --> 00:38:31.640
So what's next for Magalu?

00:38:31.640 --> 00:38:34.280
We are going to finish
the migration now

00:38:34.280 --> 00:38:37.580
of our brick-and-mortar apps.

00:38:37.580 --> 00:38:43.460
We have around 600 apps
to move in 90 days.

00:38:43.460 --> 00:38:46.400
And probably, we are going
to finish the migrate

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:48.440
by the end of May.

00:38:48.440 --> 00:38:52.790
And then we are planning to open
our APIs that we are calling

00:38:52.790 --> 00:38:55.610
our platform-- as a
Magalu as a Service

00:38:55.610 --> 00:38:59.030
and let the partners that
we have on our marketplace

00:38:59.030 --> 00:39:02.870
to use our fulfillment
centers and our stores

00:39:02.870 --> 00:39:06.170
to provide the same
experience that we

00:39:06.170 --> 00:39:09.710
are providing to our
customers for the 1P products.

00:39:09.710 --> 00:39:13.340
So they can use our fulfillment
centers to store products

00:39:13.340 --> 00:39:18.710
and use our logistics network to
delivery and same-day delivery

00:39:18.710 --> 00:39:20.990
or--

00:39:20.990 --> 00:39:23.930
actually we have 35%
of our deliveries

00:39:23.930 --> 00:39:28.400
in Brazil in the last 48 hours.

00:39:28.400 --> 00:39:31.320
And we want to provide the same
thing for these merchants that

00:39:31.320 --> 00:39:33.140
are using our platform.

00:39:33.140 --> 00:39:33.710
That's it.

00:39:33.710 --> 00:39:35.734
So back to Kiran.

00:39:35.734 --> 00:39:39.073
[APPLAUSE]

